/-!
# Derived base change for the lisse-étale and flat-fppf sites

Let `f : 𝒳 ⟶ 𝒴` be a smooth (respectively flat) 1-morphism of algebraic stacks. By
**Stacks 07AT** it restricts to a continuous and cocontinuous functor `f'` of the lisse-étale
(respectively flat-fppf) sites, so that the square of topoi

`Sh(𝒳') --g'--> Sh(𝒳_τ)`, `Sh(𝒴') --g--> Sh(𝒴_τ)`

with vertical maps `f'` and `f` commutes, `g` and `g'` being the restrictions along the
inclusions of the subsites. **Stacks 0GR3** asserts that the base change map

`g⁻¹ Rf_* ℱ ⟶ Rf'_* (g')⁻¹ ℱ`

is an isomorphism for every abelian sheaf `ℱ` on `𝒳_τ`. This file proves the passage from degree
zero to every degree: given the degree-zero identification `f_* ∘ g⁻¹ ≅ g'⁻¹ ∘ f'_*`, the higher
direct images satisfy `R^p f_* ∘ g⁻¹ ≅ g'⁻¹ ∘ R^p f'_*` for every `p`.

**Stacks 07AV** is the same square read on the *unbounded* derived categories: it asserts

`g⁻¹ ∘ Rf_* = Rf'_* ∘ (g')⁻¹` and `L(g')_! ∘ (f')⁻¹ = f⁻¹ ∘ Lg_!`.

Its abelian-sheaf case is proved here too. The first identity is the general base change theorem
`Stacks.Cohomology.UnboundedComplexes.derivedBaseChangeIso` at this square of sites, which needs
the abelian sheaves on the two sites of `𝒳` to be Grothendieck abelian — they are, both sites being
essentially small — together with the degree-zero identification and the preservation of
K-injectivity by `(g')⁻¹`. The second identity then follows formally, as the source says: the two
sides are left adjoint to the two sides of the first, so the first is the conjugate of the second
under `CategoryTheory.conjugateIsoEquiv`.

## Main definitions

* `CategoryTheory.Functor.rightDerivedCollapseIsoOfExact`: for an exact functor preserving
  injective objects, the right derived functor of a composite collapses onto the second factor.
* `CategoryTheory.Functor.rightDerivedSquareIsoOfExact`: the derived comparison of a commuting
  square of additive functors whose two horizontal functors are exact and one of which
  preserves injective objects.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.rightDerivedPushforwardRestrictionIso` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.FlatFppf.rightDerivedPushforwardRestrictionIso`: **Stacks 0GR3** for
  the lisse-étale and the flat-fppf site, in every degree, from its degree-zero case.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.derivedPushforwardRestrictionIso` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.FlatFppf.derivedPushforwardRestrictionIso`: **Stacks 07AV**, first
  identity, for abelian sheaves — `Rf'_* ∘ (g')⁻¹ ≅ g⁻¹ ∘ Rf_*` on the unbounded derived
  categories.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.derivedShriekInverseImageIso` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.FlatFppf.derivedShriekInverseImageIso`: **Stacks 07AV**, second
  identity, for abelian sheaves — `f⁻¹ ∘ Lg_! ≅ L(g')_! ∘ (f')⁻¹`, the conjugate of the first.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.isContinuous_subsiteFunctor`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.isCocontinuous_subsiteFunctor` and their flat-fppf
  counterparts: **Stacks 07AT**, the two continuity properties of the site functor, for the
  topology of the source's own definition rather than for the one generated by the chosen
  covering families.

## What remains conditional, and why

The hypothesis `hzero` is the identification `f'_*(g')⁻¹ = g⁻¹f_*` of **Stacks 07AT** (1), second
assertion. The source derives it, inside the proof of that lemma, from the two pushforward
formulas of **Stacks 06W6** and **Stacks 07AU** together with the comparison of global sections
over the 2-fibre product `V ×_{y, 𝒴} 𝒳` and over its primed full subcategory, which is an
instance of **Stacks 07AK**; the source writes "we omit the verification of the assumptions of
the lemma". Here the two pushforward formulas are
`CategoryTheory.Presheaf.pushforwardSectionsEquiv` and
`CategoryTheory.SubsitePushforward.subsitePushforwardSectionsEquiv`, but the comparison of
sections is not available: **Stacks 07AK** exists in this development only as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.cohomologyIso` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.FlatFppf.cohomologyIso`, both conditional on their own degree-zero case,
and the instance 0GR3 needs is at the localization of the subsite at the sheaf `(f')⁻¹h_y`,
which is not an object of the subsite and so is not covered by the unconditional objectwise
comparison `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.cohomologyOverObjectIso'` either.

The other hypothesis, `hinj`, is that restriction to the subsite carries injective abelian
sheaves to injective ones. It is what exactness of `g_!` gives, i.e. **Stacks 07AS** (3), which
is available in this development only conditionally
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.FlatFppf.lowerShriekAdjunction`).

Exactness of the two restrictions — **Stacks 07AS** (1) and (2) — is *not* a hypothesis. The
inclusion of each subsite is continuous and cocontinuous with no hypothesis at all, so
restriction is simultaneously the right adjoint of `g_!` and the left adjoint of `g_*`, hence
exact; `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.instAdditiveRestriction` and its three companions
seat that exactness, together with the additivity and the preservation of homology it implies,
as local instances. They apply
`Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedLowerShriek.additive_abelianRestriction` and
`Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedLowerShriek.preservesFiniteColimits_abelianRestriction` at the
coefficient universe of these sites with the universes written out, because `max v ?w` against a
concrete universe is beyond Lean's level unifier and instance search does not reach them
otherwise. Additivity of the two direct images is seated the same way, from
`CategoryTheory.Functor.sheafPushforwardCocontinuous_additive`; it cannot be an instance, since
the site functor `f'` carries a proof of smoothness (respectively flatness) that proof
irrelevance leaves undetermined in instance search.

The step this file supplies is the one 0GR3 adds over 07AT: degree zero implies every degree.
Exactness of `g⁻¹` makes right derivation commute with postcomposition by it, and exactness of
`g'⁻¹` together with `hinj` makes the right derived functor of `f'_* ∘ (g')⁻¹` collapse onto
`R^p f'_* ∘ (g')⁻¹`, so the degree-zero identification propagates.

The unbounded statements of **Stacks 07AV** carry the same `hzero`, and in place of `hinj` the
hypothesis `hKinj`: that `(g')⁻¹` carries K-injective cochain complexes to K-injective ones. That
is **Stacks 08BJ** for the adjunction `g'_! ⊣ (g')⁻¹`, hence again exactness of `g'_!`, i.e.
**Stacks 07AS** (3). They also carry the derived functors as data — a right derived functor is
determined only up to a canonical isomorphism, so `Rf_*` and `Rf'_*` come with their comparisons
from the degreewise direct images and with the `IsRightDerivedFunctor` property, as in
**Stacks 0FN6** and **Stacks 0FN7**. The second identity carries in addition the four derived
adjunctions `Lg_! ⊣ g⁻¹`, `L(g')_! ⊣ (g')⁻¹` — these are **Stacks 07AS** (3), available in this
development only conditionally — and `f⁻¹ ⊣ Rf_*`, `(f')⁻¹ ⊣ Rf'_*`; nothing more is used about
them than adjointness, which is all the source's formal argument needs.

Grothendieck abelianness of the abelian sheaves on the four sites is *not* a hypothesis: each
site is essentially small in the universe of its coefficients, so
`CategoryTheory.Sheaf.isGrothendieckAbelian_of_essentiallySmall` applies. For the fppf and
flat-fppf sites this is already
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.FlatFppf.isGrothendieckAbelian_sheaf_fppfTopology` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.FlatFppf.isGrothendieckAbelian_sheaf_inclusionTopology`; for the étale
and lisse-étale sites it is seated here, as the local instances
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.isGrothendieckAbelian_sheaf_associatedTopology` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.LisseEtale.isGrothendieckAbelian_sheaf_inclusionTopology`. The module
case of 07AV is not proved in this file.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 06W6](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06W6)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07AK](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07AK)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07AS](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07AS)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07AT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07AT)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07AU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07AU)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07AV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07AV)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08BJ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08BJ)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FN6](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FN6)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FN7](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FN7)
